\section{Setting up the diagnostic quizzes, literately}
\label{app:quiz}

This appendix is a literate program \autocite{LiterateProgramming}: the
prose and the code are one and the same source, woven into the text you
are reading and tangled into the files the computer runs.
It implements the pre/post-test of \cref{sec:method-instruments}: two
quizzes that collect two kinds of data before and after the Git module.
The closed items measure whether the students discern the candidate
critical aspects of \cref{sec:model,sec:staging,sec:branches,sec:remotes};
the open items collect the students' own accounts of what Git is, of a
commit, of recording one change and not another, of a branch, of a
history and of a shared repository, from which the phenomenographic
analysis of \cref{sec:method-instruments} forms its categories of
description.
Three files are tangled from this section:
[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]] and [[quiz-knowledge-end.json]], the
descriptions of the two quizzes that the [[canvaslms]] tool pushes to
Canvas, and [[analyze_quiz.py]], which fetches the results from Canvas,
summarises the closed items and writes the open accounts to a coding
sheet.

\subsection{The two quizzes for Canvas}

The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates the quizzes from their JSON descriptions:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms modules list -c datintro26        # read the live order first
canvaslms modules create -c datintro26 -p 5 "Git pre-test"
canvaslms modules create -c datintro26 -p 7 "Git post-test"
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz-knowledge-start.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz-knowledge-end.json
canvaslms modules edit -c datintro26 \
  --prerequisite "^The terminal$" --prerequisite "^Git pre-test$" \
  "^Collaboration$"
canvaslms modules edit -c datintro26 \
  --prerequisite "^Collaboration$" "^Git post-test$"
\end{minted}
Each quiz is the only item of a module of its own, created first: the
pre-test module sits just before the module that teaches Git, which in
this course is called \enquote{Collaboration} and also holds the pair
programming pages and the SSH-key set-up for the course's GitHub, and the
post-test module just after it.
The reason is that Canvas locks a module behind the completion of the
modules it names as prerequisites, and only whole modules can be
prerequisites.
With the pre-test in its own module, the Git module can require it, so
that no student reads the material before writing the pre-test; with the
post-test in its own module, it can require the Git module, so that no
student writes the post-test before the material.
The last two commands set up exactly that chain.
Two details of the tool matter here.
The positions are those of the live module list at the time of
creation---the terminal and report-writing modules around this one have
their own pre- and post-test modules, so the list is read before the
numbers are chosen, and [[modules list]] prints a newly created module
at the top of its output whatever its position, so the positions are
trusted over the print order.
And [[modules edit --prerequisite]] \emph{replaces} the prerequisite
list, so the Git module's existing prerequisite, the terminal module, is
restated alongside the new one.
The placement is part of the quiz description, under the key
[[modules]], which [[canvaslms]] reads and strips before it talks to
Canvas: a module name, anchored so that the pre-test module cannot match
the post-test module, the position within it, and the completion
requirement---submitting the quiz---without which a module counts as
complete the moment it is opened and the prerequisite would lock
nothing.
The modules and the quizzes are created unpublished; the teacher
releases them when the course opens.
Later changes to the items are pushed with [[canvaslms quizzes edit -c
datintro26 -a <quiz id> -f <json> --replace-items]]; the tool's cached
quiz listing does not see a quiz created by the tool itself until the
cache is bypassed with [[--no-cache]] (canvaslms issue~425), so that
flag goes on the command.
Before the course was touched, the same commands were run against a
sandbox course and the settings read back through the New Quizzes
API, which is how a quiz's result view is checked rather than assumed
(the settings below explain why that matters); the sandbox copies were
then deleted.
The two quizzes share their open items and, with one exception explained
with the item (\cref{app:knowledge-items}), their knowledge items; they
differ in what opens them.
The start-of-course quiz measures what the students bring to the module;
the end-of-course quiz repeats the same items to measure the gain, and
adds one question of its own---whether the student prepared---because
preparation would confound that gain: a student who reviews the material
the night before measures preparation, not what the module left behind.
The quiz titles name Git explicitly, since the course runs the terminal
and report-writing diagnostics as well, which must stay distinguishable
for students and analysis alike.
The instructions warn that some questions ask for an account in the
student's own words, so that a student who expects only multiple choice
does not skip them as odd.

<<[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Git quiz: what you know already",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz checks what you already know about Git, so that we can adapt the course and, together with the quiz at the end of the module, measure how well the material works. If you have not used Git before, answer as well as you can&mdash;guessing is fine, and a low score is expected and completely okay. Six questions ask you to write in your own words; there are no wrong answers there, and they carry no points. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers&mdash;the course will teach you those. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 11,
    <<start-quiz module>>
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<start result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<start-quiz consent item>>,
    <<open items>>,
    <<knowledge items>>
  ]
}
@

The end quiz differs in its opener, its instructions, and in carrying
the one knowledge item the start quiz leaves out
(\cref{app:knowledge-items}).

<<[[quiz-knowledge-end.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Git quiz: what you know now",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz repeats the Git questions from the start-of-course quiz, so that we can see what you have learned during the course. Six questions ask you to write in your own words; there are no wrong answers there, and they carry no points. Afterwards you will see your score and, for each question, the correct answer. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without preparation, help or tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 12,
    <<end-quiz module>>
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<end result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<end-quiz preparation item>>,
    <<open items>>,
    <<item: what a commit keeps>>,
    <<knowledge items>>
  ]
}
@

The module keys name the modules created above; the quiz is the first
and only item of its module, and the module is complete once the quiz
is submitted.

<<start-quiz module>>=
"modules": [{"module": "^Git pre-test$", "position": 1,
             "completion_requirement": "must_submit"}],
<<end-quiz module>>=
"modules": [{"module": "^Git post-test$", "position": 1,
             "completion_requirement": "must_submit"}],
@

Both quizzes are diagnostics: there is no time limit, a single attempt
suffices, and answers are never shuffled (several items show a sequence
of commands whose order is meaningful).
The items are shown one at a time, without going back, for a reason
that \cref{app:open-items} spells out: the open accounts must be
written before the student has seen the alternatives of the closed
items, and on a single page the order of the items would govern only the
order of reading.
The cost of forbidding revisits is small in a zero-stakes diagnostic; it
is the price of clean accounts.
The shared settings come in two chunks---scheduling and interaction.
Canvas keeps two groups of settings in objects of their own,
[[multiple_attempts]] and [[result_view_settings]], and silently ignores
the same keys written flat; so the interaction chunk carries the
attempts object whole, and the result-view chunks below are each a
complete [[result_view_settings]] object.
(The companion paper's quizzes were first deployed with the keys flat,
and the pre-test would have shown the correct answers; reading the
settings back from Canvas after deploying is what caught it, and is done
here before the real course is touched.)
Every brace still opens and closes in the chunk that writes it: the
[[quiz_settings]] object opens and closes in the quiz descriptions
above.

<<scheduling settings>>=
"time_limit": null,
"due_at": null,
"unlock_at": null,
"lock_at": null,
<<interaction settings>>=
"shuffle_answers": false,
"shuffle_questions": false,
"has_time_limit": false,
"one_at_a_time_type": "question",
"allow_backtracking": false,
"multiple_attempts": {"multiple_attempts_enabled": false},
@

The two quizzes differ in what they show the student afterwards.
The start quiz shows the points awarded---how well the student did---but
neither per-item correctness nor the correct answers: the same questions
must measure the gain at the end of the module, so they have to stay
fresh.
The end quiz has nothing left to protect, and the students have earned
the answers: it shows, item by item, whether the answer was right, which
alternative was, and a short account of why---the item's
\emph{feedback}, written with each item below---so that the diagnostic
also teaches: the distractor a student chose is the misconception they
can now read against the correct account.
For the open items the feedback is a provisional outcome space
(\cref{app:open-items}), shown for the same reason.
The feedback travels with the items into both quizzes, since the item
chunks are shared; one flag, [[display_item_feedback]], hides it in the
start quiz and shows it in the end quiz, exactly as for correctness and
the correct answer.

<<start result view>>=
"result_view_settings": {
  "result_view_restricted": true,
  "display_items": true,
  "display_item_response": true,
  "display_item_response_correctness": false,
  "display_item_correct_answer": false,
  "display_item_feedback": false,
  "display_points_awarded": true,
  "display_points_possible": true
}
<<end result view>>=
"result_view_settings": {
  "result_view_restricted": true,
  "display_items": true,
  "display_item_response": true,
  "display_item_response_correctness": true,
  "display_item_correct_answer": true,
  "display_item_feedback": true,
  "display_points_awarded": true,
  "display_points_possible": true
}
@

\subsection{The consent and preparation items}

The start-of-course quiz opens with the consent item---the same wording
as the course's terminal diagnostic, so that a student who took that one
recognises it: participation in the study is opt-in, so we ask before
collecting anything.
The item titles double as the keys under which Canvas reports the
answers, so the analysis program (\cref{app:quiz-analysis}) refers to
the items by these titles.

