\subsection{The open accounts}
\label{app:open-items}

The open items are the phenomenographic data: the students' own
accounts of what Git is and what happens when it is asked to keep their
work, of what a commit holds, of recording one change but not another,
of what a branch is, of a history with a merge in it, and of a commit
that a teammate's fresh clone does not contain.
Every student writes about the same stimulus, so the accounts are
comparable across students and between the start and the end of the
module; they are read for qualitatively different ways of experiencing
the phenomenon, sorted into categories of description and ordered into
an outcome space (\cref{sec:method-instruments}).
The stems present a concrete situation and ask for the student's own
account; none of them names an aspect, for the same reason as the closed
items below \autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}: a stem that says \enquote{snapshot},
\enquote{staging area} or \enquote{pointer} has already done the
discerning, and so, more subtly, has one that says \enquote{the remote}
where the student might have said \enquote{GitHub} or \enquote{the
server}.
The closing invitation---\enquote{if you do not know the commands,
describe in words}---matters at the start of the module, where many
students have never used Git: an account in words of what they would
want to happen is exactly the data we are after, and without the
invitation those students would leave the box empty.

The open items come \emph{before} the closed items.
The closed items' distractors spell out the candidate conceptions in
full---\enquote{only the lines you changed}, \enquote{go into the
feature directory}, \enquote{saved to the one true copy on a
server}---so a student who has just read a dozen such items has been
handed the outcome space, and an account written afterwards echoes the
alternatives rather than the student's own way of seeing.
The other direction is harmless: an open stem shows only a situation,
and whatever small effect writing about it has on the closed answers is
the same in both administrations, so the pre/post gain is unaffected.
This is why the quizzes show one item at a time without backtracking
(\cref{app:quiz}).

<<open items>>=
<<item: what Git is>>,
<<item: what Git has kept>>,
<<item: keeping one of two changes>>,
<<item: the branch called feature>>,
<<item: explaining a history>>,
<<item: the teammate's clone>>
@

All open items carry no points and no correct answer; Canvas's essay
interaction needs only the flag that turns off the rich-text editor (a
plain box invites plain prose) and the word limit.
The items occupy positions 3--8, leaving position 2 free for a further
opening item, just as the knowledge items leave room below 11.
Three of the six are \emph{open twins} of closed items
(\cref{app:knowledge-items}): the same situation, but no alternatives,
so that the account and the choice of the same student can be compared,
and so that an aspect whose closed alternatives would teach it can still
be asked about at the start.
A twin's title avoids its closed partner's title as a substring, since
the analysis (\cref{app:quiz-analysis}) finds the report columns by
title.

The first item asks for the student's conception of Git itself and of
what happens when Git is asked to keep the work.
It is the counterpart of the opening question of the companion paper's
terminal quiz, \enquote{what is the terminal, and what is it for?}
\autocite{VTTerminal}; there is no phenomenographic study of Git to
follow (\cref{sec:protocol-measurement}).
The stem says \enquote{keep your work as it is now}, not
\enquote{commit} or \enquote{save a version}: both words name what the
commit-model aspects of \cref{sec:model} ask the student to
discern---that recording a version is an act distinct from saving, and
that what is recorded is the whole project as it stands---and a stem
that supplies either would be doing the discerning
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.

<<item: what Git is>>=
{
  "position": 3,
  "points_possible": 0,
  "entry": {
    "title": "What Git is",
    "item_body": "<p>Imagine explaining to a friend who has never used it: what is Git, and what is it for? And when you have changed some files and want Git to keep your work as it is now&mdash;what do you do, and what happens? Write in your own words; there is no right length.</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "essay",
    "interaction_data": {"rce": false, "word_limit_enabled": false},
    "scoring_data": {"value": ""},
    "scoring_algorithm": "None",
    <<feedback: what Git is>>
  }
}
@

The second item is the open twin of the closed item \emph{what a commit
keeps}, which the start quiz leaves out because its alternatives spell
out the snapshot model (\cref{app:knowledge-items}).
It shows a small report, an edit to one file, and the two commands that
record it, and asks what Git now holds and what a co-author would receive
with that commit.
The second question is there to make the account concrete: a student
who pictures the commit as the changed lines, as the one file, or as the
whole project will say so when asked what arrives at the other end.
The commands are shown rather than described so that the student who has
never typed them can still answer from what they imagine the commands
do; the stem does not say what a commit \emph{is}
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.

<<item: what Git has kept>>=
{
  "position": 4,
  "points_possible": 0,
  "entry": {
    "title": "What Git has kept",
    "item_body": "<p>A report has three files: intro.txt, method.txt and results.txt. You edit method.txt and then run:</p><pre>$ git add method.txt\n$ git commit -m \"Describe the sample\"</pre><p>What does Git now hold on to, and what would a co-author receive who later gets that commit from you? Write in your own words; if you do not know the commands, describe what you would expect to happen.</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "essay",
    "interaction_data": {"rce": false, "word_limit_enabled": false},
    "scoring_data": {"value": ""},
    "scoring_algorithm": "None",
    <<feedback: what Git has kept>>
  }
}
@

The third item is a task rather than a definition question, for the
same reason the terminal quiz asks how to get a file into place rather
than what a directory is \autocite{VTTerminal}: a definition question
invites the textbook phrase, a task invites the student's picture of how
the system works.
The task---record the change to one file now, but not the change to the
other---is the one the staging area exists for (\cref{sec:staging}), and
a student who has not discerned the index will either see no way to do
it, or commit both, or describe copying the file aside; all three are
accounts we want.
The stem names neither \enquote{stage}, \enquote{index} nor
\enquote{add}, so that the discernment is the student's
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.

