The fourth item is the open twin of the closed item \emph{seeing
another branch}: a teammate's remark that their work is on a branch, and
two questions---what the student pictures a branch to be, and what they
would do to look at the work.
The first question is the phenomenographic one---what the student
understands a branch to be, asked as the companion paper's interview
prompts ask about the shell's objects \autocite{VTTerminal}; the second
is where the branch-as-directory conception (\cref{sec:branches}) shows
itself unprompted, as a \texttt{cd}, an \enquote{open the folder}, or a
search for the files somewhere on disk.
What the account is read for is the essence of the aspect: whether the
student sees the project as existing in several concurrent versions, of
which the branch names one and the working tree shows one at a time.
A folder picture is one way of not seeing that; it may also be a correct
picture of another system carried over, since in Subversion a branch
\emph{is} a directory in the repository \autocite[Ch.~4]{SVNBook}, so
an account that says \enquote{the branches folder} is a category to
look for, not only an error.
The word \emph{directory} and the word \emph{folder} are kept out of the
stem, since either would hand over the comparison the closed item's
alternatives make \autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.

