The fifth item shows a small history with a merge in it, as
[[git log --oneline --graph]] draws it, and asks what it shows.
This is the commit graph itself (\cref{sec:model,sec:branches}) put in
front of the student, and the account tells how they read it: as a list
of saves, as two folders that were combined, as two lines of work that
were joined.
The stem glosses nothing---not the stars, not the lines, not
\enquote{merge}---because the reading is the data; a student who has
never seen such a picture will say what they make of it, and that too
is an account.

