\subsubsection{What the end quiz shows for an account}

The end quiz shows the student, after submission, an \enquote{answer}
to every item, the open ones included (\cref{app:quiz}).
For an open item that answer cannot be a correct account; it is a
\emph{provisional outcome space}: three or four one-sentence ways of
seeing the phenomenon, ordered from a first impression to the way of
seeing the module aims at, each later one discerning something the
earlier one does not---which is what orders an outcome space
\autocite[Ch.~2]{NCOL}.
They are posited from the candidate aspects of the chapters, since no
student accounts exist yet; after the first cohort they are to be
replaced by the empirical outcome space that the coding of the accounts
produces.
% TODO(#8): replace the provisional outcome spaces with the empirical ones
% after cohort 1, re-tangle and re-sync the end quiz.
Two things follow from their purpose.
They are shown only in the end quiz and only after submission, so no
account is written with them in view: the start quiz hides item feedback
(\cref{app:quiz}), and the accounts of the end quiz are in before the
student sees anything.
And they are not given to the coder, nor to the pre-coding prompt of
\cref{app:quiz-analysis}, for the same reason the prompt offers no list:
the categories are what the analysis is to find, and a list handed to
the coder would turn finding into confirming.
Canvas carries the text as the item's \emph{feedback}, the same field
that holds the account of why for the closed items below, so one
setting switches all of them.

<<feedback: what Git is>>=
"feedback": {"neutral": "<p>Different answers to this question show different ways of seeing this. In order, from a first impression to the way of seeing the module aims at:</p><ol><li>Git is a place to store or back up files&mdash;saving to the cloud.</li><li>Git keeps versions of files, so that an old one can be got back.</li><li>Git records the whole project as a series of versions that you choose to record, each a snapshot linked to the one before.</li><li>&hellip;and several people can each hold a complete copy of that history and exchange versions through a shared repository.</li></ol>"}
<<feedback: what Git has kept>>=
"feedback": {"neutral": "<p>Different answers to this question show different ways of seeing this. In order, from a first impression to the way of seeing the module aims at:</p><ol><li>Git kept the edit you made&mdash;the changed lines.</li><li>Git kept the new version of method.txt.</li><li>Git kept the whole report as it stood at that moment, all three files; a co-author who gets the commit gets that whole version.</li><li>&hellip;together with the link to the version before, so they can see what changed and go back to it.</li></ol>"}
<<feedback: keeping one of two changes>>=
"feedback": {"neutral": "<p>Different answers to this question show different ways of seeing this. In order, from a first impression to the way of seeing the module aims at:</p><ol><li>It cannot be done, since Git records everything that changed&mdash;or you would copy data.csv aside first.</li><li>You tell Git which file to record by naming it when you commit.</li><li>You first mark (stage) the change to notes.txt with git add notes.txt and then commit; the change to data.csv stays in your folder, unrecorded, and git status shows both states.</li></ol>"}
<<feedback: the branch called feature>>=
"feedback": {"neutral": "<p>Different answers to this question show different ways of seeing this. In order, from a first impression to the way of seeing the module aims at:</p><ol><li>A branch is a folder or a copy of the project somewhere, and you would go into it or open it.</li><li>A branch is a separate version of the project; you switch to it and your folder shows that version.</li><li>A branch is a name for one of several concurrent lines of the same project's history; switching (git switch feature) makes your one working folder show that line, and the lines can later be joined by a merge.</li></ol>"}
<<feedback: explaining a history>>=
"feedback": {"neutral": "<p>Different answers to this question show different ways of seeing this. In order, from a first impression to the way of seeing the module aims at:</p><ol><li>A list of saves, newest at the top.</li><li>Two lines of work happened at the same time and were put together.</li><li>From &ldquo;First draft&rdquo; the project went two ways at once&mdash;one line wrote the results, the other fixed the title&mdash;each commit pointing back at the one before; the merge commit at the top joins the two lines again, and the stars and lines draw exactly that graph.</li></ol>"}
