\subsection{The knowledge items}
\label{app:knowledge-items}

The knowledge items are written for this study and published here in
full; no Git instrument existed to borrow from
(\cref{sec:protocol-measurement}).
Each item tests one candidate critical aspect from the chapters: whether
the student discerns it is exactly what the item measures.
Following \citeauthor{NCOL}'s discussion of assessment, the stems do not
point out the relevant aspect---discerning it is what we measure
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}---so each stem shows a concrete command and its
behaviour, or a concrete situation, and leaves the interpretation to the
student.
Each distractor expresses a documented misconception or a plausible
consequence of leaving the aspect undiscerned; the prose before each
item names the aspect it tests and why the distractors are the mistakes
they are, and cites the source of a documented one.
The same account, in plain words and no longer than the item, follows
each item as its feedback: the end quiz shows it with the correct
alternative (\cref{app:quiz}), so the distractor a student chose is the
misconception they can now read against it.
The correct alternative is kept about as long as the distractors, since
the longest alternative is a known cue for test-wise students.
The files in the stems are the chapters and notes of a report
([[intro.txt]], [[method.txt]], [[notes.txt]]), not source code: the
course teaches Git to students who have not necessarily met a
programming language, and a stem in Python would ask them to discern
the commit model through an unfamiliar artefact; a report is what they
will version in the course.
The same holds for the open items above.
All items weigh one point: we measure knowledge by counting correct
answers, and \cref{app:quiz-analysis} reports them item by item, since
each item stands for one aspect.

The items are numbered from position 11, so that their placement is
independent of the quiz they appear in: Canvas orders by position, gaps
are fine, and the numbering leaves room for the quiz-specific opening
item (consent or preparation) and the open accounts before them.
They come in chapter order---the commit model, the staging area,
branches, remotes---and within a chapter in the order in which the
aspects build on each other; where one item would make the next one
answerable by comparison, the item that stands on its own comes first.

<<knowledge items>>=
<<item: after you save>>,
<<item: where earlier versions live>>,
<<item: what git add does>>,
<<item: what status shows after an edit>>,
<<item: two versions at once>>,
<<item: seeing another branch>>,
<<item: deleting a branch>>,
<<item: what merging does>>,
<<item: offline with a clone>>,
<<item: what git push sends>>,
<<item: why they cannot see it>>
@

\subsubsection{The commit model}

A commit is a snapshot of the whole project, not a stored difference
(\cref{sec:model}).
The item shows a three-file report, an edit to one file, and the
[[add]]/[[commit]] pair that records it, and asks what the commit
records.
The correct answer is the whole report as it stood; the leading
distractor is the commit-as-diff reading---only the changed lines---and
the next is the partial-snapshot reading that only the edited file is
kept; the last defers recording to the push.
This item is the one the start quiz leaves out: there the same
situation is the open item \emph{what Git has kept}
(\cref{app:open-items}), because the alternatives spell out the
snapshot model---the whole project, every file, with the edited one in
its new state---to students who may be meeting Git for the first time,
and the start quiz promises not to reveal what the course will teach.
The end quiz carries both forms, so for this aspect the movement is read
from the account at the start to the item at the end, and the paired
per-item gain of \cref{app:quiz-analysis} is computed over the other
items only.
The item keeps position 11 in the end quiz; the start quiz simply has a
gap there.

<<item: what a commit keeps>>=
{
