The end quiz shows, with the correct alternative, this account of why:

<<feedback: what a commit keeps>>=
"feedback": {"neutral": "<p>A commit records the whole report as it stood&mdash;every file, not only the one you edited and not only the changed lines; the difference between two versions is something Git computes when asked, not what it stores. And a commit exists the moment you make it; pushing only sends it to another copy of the repository.</p>"}
@


Recording a version is a separate act from saving a file
(\cref{sec:model}).
The item shows a one-line history, an edit saved in the editor, no Git
commands, and asks what the history shows now.
The correct answer is the same one line; the leading distractor is the
commit-as-save conflation---the saved version was added to the
history---which repository-data studies find students hold, treating
the system as file storage \autocite{Cochez2013DVCSUsage}; the others
have saving overwrite the recorded version, or record a draft.
The stem shows [[git log]] rather than asking \enquote{what is true of
the history}, so that the student reads a concrete screen
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}.

