[[git status]] reads the three places---working tree, index,
history---and reports a file by where it differs, not by whether it is
saved (\cref{sec:staging}).
The item shows a clean status after a commit, then one saved edit, and
asks what status shows now.
The correct answer puts the file under \enquote{not staged for
commit}, because the saved file differs from what was last committed;
the leading distractor keeps the tree clean because the file is saved
(saving as the end of the story, the conflation of \cref{sec:model} seen
from the index), the next has saving stage the change, and the last
demands an [[add]] before status can read the file.
This item comes before \emph{two versions at once}: shown after a status
with a file in both lists, the single-list answer would be wrong by
inspection, and the item would measure comparison rather than
discernment.

