Push moves commits, not files: what arrives at the other copy is the
history since the last push, and an uncommitted edit stays behind
(\cref{sec:remotes}).
The item gives two commits and an uncommitted edit after them and asks
what the push carries.
The correct answer is the two commits; the leading distractor carries
the edit along, the next is push-as-upload of the working folder, the
save-to-the-cloud picture that the basic-workflow misconception
catalogue targets \autocite{Postner2026GitMisconceptions}, and the last
has the push replace the remote wholesale.
An exam item of this shape---whether a pull modifies the local
directory, the remote, both or neither---is how Git is assessed where it
is a stated learning objective \autocite{Beckman2021GitLearningObjective}.

