        {"position": 2, "item_body": "<p>The two commits and the uncommitted edit, since push sends everything new</p>"},
        {"position": 3, "item_body": "<p>All your files as they are on disk right now, as when uploading to a cloud drive</p>"},
        {"position": 4, "item_body": "<p>Your whole project folder, replacing what was on the remote before</p>"}
      ]
    },
    "scoring_data": {"value": 1},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence",
    <<feedback: what git push sends>>
  }
}
@
The end quiz shows, with the correct alternative, this account of why:

<<feedback: what git push sends>>=
"feedback": {"neutral": "<p>Push sends commits: the history you have recorded since the last push. An edit you have not committed is not part of any commit, so it stays on your computer. Push is not an upload of your working folder, and it does not replace the remote; it adds your commits to it.</p>"}
@


Local commits are invisible to others until pushed, and vice versa
(\cref{sec:remotes}).
The key distractor is the expectation that a commit is visible to
everyone as soon as it is made---the confusion about the distributed
state that course observations document \autocite{IsomottonenCochez2014}
and that the split-view tool sets out to make visible
\autocite{Bucher2026GitTakesTwo}; the others delay the commit in
transit or let it expire.
The stem does not say that nothing was pushed (an earlier version did),
since that sentence names the missing step the student is to supply
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}; the open twin \emph{the teammate's clone}
(\cref{app:open-items}) asks the same situation without alternatives.

